      "writtenOn": "2022-10-12T18:24:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "you may keep this implementation, but to share with you this is how the functional style implementation can look like:\n```\nfn append_uuids(dest: \u0026mut Vec\u003cu8\u003e, ad_types: (u8, u8, u8), uuids: \u0026Vec\u003cUuid\u003e) {\n    for (ad_type, uuids) in uuids\n        .iter()\n        .map(|uuid| UuidHelper::get_shortest_slice(\u0026uuid.uu))\n        .group_by(|uuid| match uuid.len() {\n            2 \u003d\u003e ad_types.0,\n            4 \u003d\u003e ad_types.1,\n            16 \u003d\u003e ad_types.2,\n            _ \u003d\u003e 0,\n        })\n        .into_iter()\n        .filter(|(k, _)| *k !\u003d 0)\n    {\n        let little_endian_uuids \u003d uuids.fold(Vec::new(), |mut acc, uuid| {\n            acc.extend(uuid.iter().rev());\n            acc\n        });\n        Self::append_adv_data(dest, ad_type, \u0026little_endian_uuids);\n    }\n}\n```\nnotice that:\n1. no unnecessary mutability\n2. no re-inventing logic (therefore actually more expressive)\n3. still efficient (can defer all array copy until the last step)\n\nIf you want to give it a try, add `itertools \u003d \"0.10.5\"` to `system/gd/rust/linux/stack/Cargo.toml` and add `use itertools::Itertools` on top of the file. The crate is already in our vendored crates so we don\u0027t need audit.\n\nIf you want to keep the imperative style, I have other comments above.",
      "range": {
